        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 976,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the dot product of vectors: each pair of entries multiplied, and the products added up. For two directions of length 1 it is the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when they point the same way, 0 at right angles, -1 when opposite.\n\nFor one direction of length 1, the dot product of any vector with it is that vector's component along it - how far the vector reaches in that direction. How high each joint is (dotted with up), how much of a hand's velocity is towards the audience, how much a limb is aligned with another.\n\nIt works along the last dim, on whole stacks at once: 24 positions (24 x 3) dotted with one direction give 24 answers; two stacks of 24 give 24 pairwise answers. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dot <dim>\nDefault: dim -1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nA vector, or a stack of them. Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe other vector or stack, held: send it first. Its shape must match in 1, or broadcast against it.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the vectors run along: -1 (the default) for N x 3 stacks, 0 for 3 x N.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndot product:\nOne number per vector (or per pair).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour directions, one per row, each dotted with up (0 1 0, held). Pointing sideways gives 0, straight up 1, halfway between 0.5, straight down -1: each direction's height.\n\nRELATED:\nt.cosine_similarity divides by both lengths, so only the angle counts. t.cross gives what two vectors do not share. t.matmul and t.einsum do the same sums for whole matrices.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 960,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the dot product of vectors: each pair of entries multiplied, and the products added up. For two directions of length 1 it is the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when they point the same way, 0 at right angles, -1 when opposite.\n\nFor one direction of length 1, the dot product of any vector with it is that vector's component along it - how far the vector reaches in that direction. How high each joint is (dotted with up), how much of a hand's velocity is towards the audience, how much a limb is aligned with another.\n\nIt works along the last dim, on whole stacks at once: 24 positions (24 x 3) dotted with one direction give 24 answers; two stacks of 24 give 24 pairwise answers. The answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.dot <dim>\nDefault: dim -1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nA vector, or a stack of them. Every input calculates and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe other vector or stack, held: send it first. Its shape must match in 1, or broadcast against it.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the vectors run along: -1 (the default) for N x 3 stacks, 0 for 3 x N.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndot product:\nOne number per vector (or per pair).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour directions, one per row, each dotted with up (0 1 0, held). Pointing sideways gives 0, straight up 1, halfway between 0.5, straight down -1: each direction's height.\n\nRELATED:\nt.cosine_similarity divides by both lengths, so only the angle counts. t.cross gives what two vectors do not share. t.matmul and t.einsum do the same sums for whole matrices."
        },
